Remote Debugging of Containers
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Set up remote debugging for containers without rebuilding images.
Expose debug ports for remote debugging.
Mount source code directory into a container for live updates.
Connect an IDE debugger (VS Code) to a running container.
Prerequisites
Basic understanding of containers and Podman/Docker.
Podman or Docker installed (Podman preferred for OpenShift compatibility).
VS Code installed with the "Remote - Containers" extension.
A sample application (e.g., a Flask or Node.js app) for debugging.
Lab Tasks
Task 1: Expose Debug Ports
Objective: Configure a container to expose debugging ports for remote connections.

Subtask 1.1: Identify Debug Port for Your Application
Different languages use different default debug ports:
Node.js: 9229 (for --inspect flag)
Python: 5678 (for debugpy)
Java: 5005 (for JDWP)
Subtask 1.2: Run Container with Debug Port Exposed
Use the following command to start a container with the debug port exposed:

podman run -d -p 3000:3000 -p 9229:9229 --name debug-container my-node-app
-p 9229:9229 exposes the Node.js debug port.
Replace my-node-app with your application image.
Expected Outcome:

The container runs with the application and debug port accessible.
Troubleshooting:

If the port is already in use, change the host port (e.g., -p 9230:9229).
Task 2: Mount Source Code for Live Debugging
Objective: Attach local source code to the container for real-time debugging.

Subtask 2.1: Run Container with Volume Mount
Use the -v flag to mount your local source code into the container:

podman run -d -p 3000:3000 -p 9229:9229 -v $(pwd):/app --name debug-container my-node-app
$(pwd):/app mounts the current directory to /app in the container.
Subtask 2.2: Verify Mounted Files
Enter the container and check if files are accessible:

podman exec -it debug-container ls /app
Expected Outcome:

You should see your local files inside the container.
Troubleshooting:

If files are missing, ensure the path is correct.
Task 3: Connect IDE Debugger to Container
Objective: Attach VS Code to the container for interactive debugging.

Subtask 3.1: Install VS Code Remote Debugging Tools
Install the Remote - Containers extension in VS Code.
For Node.js, install the Debugger for Node.js extension.
Subtask 3.2: Attach Debugger to Running Container
Open VS Code and go to the Run and Debug panel.

Click Create a launch.json file and select Node.js.

Modify the launch.json to include:

{
  "version": "0.2.0",
  "configurations": [
    {
      "type": "node",
      "request": "attach",
      "name": "Attach to Container",
      "address": "localhost",
      "port": 9229,
      "localRoot": "${workspaceFolder}",
      "remoteRoot": "/app"
    }
  ]
}
Start debugging by clicking the green play button.

Expected Outcome:

VS Code connects to the container, and breakpoints work.
Troubleshooting:

If the debugger doesn’t attach, verify the port and container status.
Conclusion
In this lab, you learned how to:

Expose debug ports in a container.
Mount source code for live debugging.
Connect VS Code to a running container for remote debugging.
This approach avoids rebuilding images and speeds up debugging workflows.

